{"revision_id":105,"created":"2018-07-30T00:00:00+00:00","metadata":{"citation_count":20,"publication_info":[{"year":2019,"page_end":"186","page_start":"145","journal_issue":"1","journal_title":"Commun.Math.Phys.","journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1214791"},"journal_volume":"374"}],"citation_count_without_self_citations":19,"dois":[{"value":"10.1007/s00220-019-03571-9","source":"bibmatch"}],"titles":[{"title":"Symmetries of Cosmological Cauchy Horizons with Non-Closed Orbits","source":"arXiv"}],"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","authors":[{"uuid":"fbcde4c6-adec-41a4-b6f3-32c421edb3e2","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/997016"},"full_name":"Moncrief, Vincent","signature_block":"MANCRAFv","raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Department of Physics and Department of Mathematics, Yale University, P.O. Box 208120, 06520 New Haven, CT, USA"}],"affiliations_identifiers":[{"value":"grid.47100.32","schema":"GRID"}]},{"ids":[{"value":"0000-0002-3470-2268","schema":"ORCID"}],"uuid":"c0b1290d-3fa8-446e-9f06-39e162e70d8f","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1004888"},"full_name":"Isenberg, James","signature_block":"ISANBARGj","raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Department of Mathematics and Institute for Theoretical Science, University of Oregon, 97403 Eugene, OR, USA"}],"affiliations_identifiers":[{"value":"grid.170202.6","schema":"GRID"}]}],"curated":false,"license":[{"url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","license":"arXiv nonexclusive-distrib 1.0","material":"preprint"}],"texkeys":["Moncrief:2018tib"],"citeable":true,"imprints":[{"date":"2019-09-18"}],"refereed":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"We consider analytic, vacuum spacetimes that admit compact, non-degenerate Cauchy horizons. Many years ago we proved that, if the null geodesic generators of such a horizon were all closed curves, then the enveloping spacetime would necessarily admit a non-trivial, horizon-generating Killing vector field. Using a slightly extended version of the Cauchy–Kowaleski theorem one could establish the existence of infinite dimensional, analytic families of such ‘generalized Taub-NUT’ spacetimes and show that, generically, they admitted only the single (horizon-generating) Killing field alluded to above. In this article we relax the closure assumption and analyze vacuum spacetimes in which the generic horizon generating null geodesic densely fills a 2-torus lying in the horizon. In particular we show that, aside from some highly exceptional cases that we refer to as ‘ergodic’, the non-closed generators always have this (densely 2-torus-filling) geometrical property in the analytic setting. By extending arguments we gave previously for the characterization of the Killing symmetries of higher dimensional, stationary black holes we prove that analytic, 4-dimensional, vacuum spacetimes with such (non-ergodic) compact Cauchy horizons always admit (at least) two independent, commuting Killing vector fields of which a special linear combination is horizon generating. We also discuss the conjectures that every such spacetime with an ergodic horizon is trivially constructable from the flat Kasner solution by making certain ‘irrational’ toroidal compactifications and that degenerate compact Cauchy horizons do not exist in the analytic case.","source":"Springer"},{"value":"We consider analytic, vacuum spacetimes that admit compact, non-degenerate Cauchy horizons. Many years ago we proved that, if the null geodesic generators of such a horizon were all \\textit{closed} curves, then the enveloping spacetime would necessarily admit a non-trivial, horizon-generating Killing vector field. Using a slightly extended version of the Cauchy-Kowaleski theorem one could establish the existence of infinite dimensional, analytic families of such `generalized Taub-NUT' spacetimes and show that, generically, they admitted \\textit{only} the single (horizon-generating) Killing field alluded to above. In this article we relax the closure assumption and analyze vacuum spacetimes in which the generic horizon generating null geodesic densely fills a 2-torus lying in the horizon. In particular we show that, aside from some highly exceptional cases that we refer to as `ergodic', the non-closed generators always have this (densely 2-torus-filling) geometrical property in the analytic setting. By extending arguments we gave previously for the characterization of the Killing symmetries of higher dimensional, stationary black holes we prove that analytic, 4-dimensional, vacuum spacetimes with such (non-ergodic) compact Cauchy horizons always admit (at least) two independent, commuting Killing vector fields of which a special linear combination is horizon generating. We also discuss the \\textit{conjectures} that every such spacetime with an \\textit{ergodic} horizon is trivially constructable from the flat Kasner solution by making certain `irrational' toroidal compactifications and that degenerate compact Cauchy horizons do not exist in the analytic case.","source":"arXiv"}],"references":[{"raw_refs":[{"value":"[1.] V Moncrief: The space of (generalized) Taub-NUT spacetimes, J. Geom. Phys. 1 (1984) 107-130, DOI: 10.1016/0393-0440(84)90016-0","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1016/0393-0440(84)90016-0"],"label":"1"}},{"raw_refs":[{"value":"[2.] V Moncrief: Neighborhoods of Cauchy horizons in cosmological spacetimes with one Killing field, Ann. Phys. 141 (1982) 83-103, DOI: 10.1016/0003-4916(82)90273-1","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1016/0003-4916(82)90273-1"],"label":"2"}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/583970"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"[3.] J Isenberg, V Moncrief: Asymptotic behaviour in polarized and half-polarized U(1) symmetric vacuum spacetimes, Class. Quantum Gravity 19, No. 21 (2002) 5361-5386, DOI: 10.1088/0264-9381/19/21/305","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1088/0264-9381/19/21/305"],"label":"3"},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/664814"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"[4.] Y Choquet-Bruhat, J Isenberg, V Moncrief: Topologically general U(1) symmetric vacuum space–times with AVTD behavior, Nuovo Cimento Soc. Ital. Fis. B 119, No. 7–9 (2004) 625-638","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"misc":["[4.]",": Topologically general U(1) symmetric vacuum space-times with AVTD behavior","625-638"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Choquet-Bruhat, Y."},{"full_name":"Isenberg, J."},{"full_name":"Moncrief, V."}],"publication_info":{"year":2004,"artid":"9","page_start":"9","journal_title":"Nuovo Cim.B","journal_volume":"119"}},"curated_relation":false},{"raw_refs":[{"value":"[5.] Y Choquet-Bruhat, J Isenberg: Half polarized U(1) symmetric vacuum spacetimes with AVTD behavior, J. Geom. Phys. 56, No. 8 (2006) 1199-1214, DOI: 10.1016/j.geomphys.2005.06.11","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1016/j.geomphys.2005.06.11"],"label":"5"}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1235595"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"[6.] V Moncrief, J Isenberg: Symmetries of cosmological Cauchy horizons, Commun. Math. Phys. 89 (1983) 387-413, DOI: 10.1007/BF01214662","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1007/BF01214662"],"label":"6"},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2862908"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"[7.] J Isenberg, V Moncrief: Symmetries of cosmological Cauchy horizons with exceptional orbits, J. Math. Phys. 26 (1985) 1024-1027, DOI: 10.1063/1.526587","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1063/1.526587"],"label":"7"}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2862904"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"[8.] J Isenberg, V Moncrief: On spacetimes containing Killing vector fields with non-closed orbits, Class. Quantum Gravity 9 (1992) 1683-1691, DOI: 10.1088/0264-9381/9/7/004","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1088/0264-9381/9/7/004"],"label":"8"}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/311851"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"[9.] PT Chruściel, J Isenberg, V Moncrief: Strong cosmic censorship in polarised Gowdy spacetimes, Class. Quantum Gravity 7, No. 10 (1990) 1671-1680, DOI: 10.1088/0264-9381/7/10/003","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1088/0264-9381/7/10/003"],"label":"9"},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/785484"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"[10.] V Moncrief, J Isenberg: Symmetries of higher dimensional black holes, Class. Quantum Gravity 25 (2008) 195015, DOI: 10.1088/0264-9381/25/19/195015","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1088/0264-9381/25/19/195015"],"label":"10"},"curated_relation":false},{"raw_refs":[{"value":"[11.] B Coll: On the evolution equations for Killing fields, J. Math. Phys. 18 (1977) 1918-1922, DOI: 10.1063/1.523164","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1063/1.523164"],"label":"11"}},{"raw_refs":[{"value":"[12.] S Hawking, G Ellis: The Large Scale Structure of Space-Time, Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 1973","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"misc":["[12.]",": Cambridge University Press, Cambridge"],"title":{"title":"The Large scale structure of space-time"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Hawking, S."},{"full_name":"Ellis, G."}],"publication_info":{"year":1973}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/717366"},"raw_refs":[{"value":"[13.] S Hollands, A Ishibashi, R Wald: A higher dimensional stationary rotating black hole must be axisymmetric, Commun. Math. Phys. 271 (2007) 699-722, DOI: 10.1007/s00220-007-0216-4","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"dois":["10.1007/s00220-007-0216-4"],"label":"13"},"curated_relation":false},{"raw_refs":[{"value":"[14.] H Poincaré: Les Méthodes Nouvelles de la Mécanique Classique Céleste, Gauthiers-Villars, Paris, 1899","schema":"text"}],"reference":{"misc":["[14.]",": Les Méthodes Nouvelles de la Mécanique Classique Céleste, Gauthiers-Villars, Paris, 1899"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Poincaré, H."}]}},{"raw_r